{"id":"chokes-names-and-marks","rev":2,"lang":"en","status":"published","review_flag":null,"disclaimer":null,"title":"Choke names, constrictions and markings","summary":"From cylinder to full: typical constrictions in millimetres and thou, the 1/4–1/2–3/4 fractions and the star marks used in Sporting™ and Compak® Sporting. Values are approximate and differ between makers.","body":[{"k":"gear-chokes-p3","t":"p","html":"Names come from English, from open to tight: cylinder, improved cylinder, light modified, modified, improved modified, full. Europeans often say them as fractions: quarter (improved cylinder), half (modified), three-quarter (improved modified) and full. Many Italian guns mark chokes with stars, and there the rule is upside down: more stars means a more open choke.","disc":null,"rule_ref":null},{"k":"dg-choke-scale","t":"diagram","html":"<figure class=\"dg\" aria-labelledby=\"dg-choke-scale\"><p class=\"dg-h\" id=\"dg-choke-scale\">Chokes from open to tight</p><svg class=\"dg-svg\" viewBox=\"20 20 640 300\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"dg-choke-scale\" focusable=\"false\"><rect class=\"dg-bar0\" x=\"104\" y=\"167\" width=\"52\" height=\"3\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"dg-val\" x=\"130\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0 mm</text><text class=\"dg-nm\" x=\"130\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cyl</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">—</text><text class=\"dg-star\" x=\"130\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">★★★★★</text><rect class=\"dg-bar1\" x=\"196\" y=\"140\" width=\"52\" height=\"30\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"dg-val\" x=\"222\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈0.25 mm</text><text class=\"dg-nm\" x=\"222\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">IC</text><text x=\"222\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1/4</text><text class=\"dg-star\" x=\"222\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">★★★★</text><rect class=\"dg-bar2\" x=\"288\" y=\"125\" width=\"52\" height=\"45\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"dg-val\" x=\"314\" y=\"117\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈0.35–0.4</text><text class=\"dg-nm\" x=\"314\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">LM</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3/8</text><text class=\"dg-star\" x=\"314\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">—</text><rect class=\"dg-bar3\" x=\"380\" y=\"110\" width=\"52\" height=\"60\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"dg-val\" x=\"406\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈0.5 mm</text><text class=\"dg-nm\" x=\"406\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">M</text><text x=\"406\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1/2</text><text class=\"dg-star\" x=\"406\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">★★★</text><rect class=\"dg-bar4\" x=\"472\" y=\"86\" width=\"52\" height=\"84\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"dg-val\" x=\"498\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈0.65–0.75</text><text class=\"dg-nm\" x=\"498\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">IM</text><text x=\"498\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3/4</text><text class=\"dg-star\" x=\"498\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">★★</text><rect class=\"dg-bar5\" x=\"564\" y=\"56\" width=\"52\" height=\"114\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"dg-val\" x=\"590\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈0.9–1 mm</text><text class=\"dg-nm\" x=\"590\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Full</text><text x=\"590\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1/1</text><text class=\"dg-star\" x=\"590\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\">★</text>\n<path class=\"dg-base\" d=\"M96 170H594\"/><path class=\"dg-arr\" d=\"M110 296H580\"/><path class=\"dg-arrh\" d=\"M580 289l14 7-14 7z\"/><g class=\"dg-n\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"120\" r=\"14\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\">1</text></g><g class=\"dg-n\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"195\" r=\"14\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\">2</text></g><g class=\"dg-n\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"231\" r=\"14\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"231\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\">3</text></g><g class=\"dg-n\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"265\" r=\"14\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\">4</text></g><g class=\"dg-n\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"296\" r=\"14\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"296\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\">5</text></g></svg><ol class=\"dg-leg\"><li><b aria-hidden=\"true\">1</b>Constriction in millimetres (about)</li><li><b aria-hidden=\"true\">2</b>The English name</li><li><b aria-hidden=\"true\">3</b>The fraction Europeans say</li><li><b aria-hidden=\"true\">4</b>Italian star marks: more stars, a more open choke</li><li><b aria-hidden=\"true\">5</b>From open to tight</li></ol><figcaption>Typical constrictions for 12 gauge; real values depend on the maker and the barrel. LM has no star mark in this scheme.</figcaption></figure>","disc":null,"rule_ref":null},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-p1","t":"p","html":"Below are typical <a class=\"term\" href=\"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/constriction/\">constrictions</a> for 12 gauge. One thou is a thousandth of an inch, about 0.025 mm. Real values depend on the maker and even on the bore of the particular barrel.","disc":null,"rule_ref":null},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-p2","t":"steps","html":["Cylinder: no constriction, 0 mm.","1/8: a very light constriction, about 0.1–0.15 mm (4–6 thou), between cylinder and improved cylinder; makers name it differently.","Improved cylinder (IC): about 0.25 mm (around 10 thou), in Europe 1/4, a quarter choke.","Light modified (LM): about 0.35–0.4 mm (around 15 thou), sometimes marked 3/8.","Modified (M): about 0.5 mm (around 20 thou), 1/2, a half choke.","Improved modified (IM): about 0.65–0.75 mm (25–30 thou), 3/4.","Full: about 0.9–1 mm (35–40 thou), 1/1 or simply Full. Tighter chokes (extra full) exist, but clay shooting rarely needs them."],"disc":null,"rule_ref":null},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-p5","t":"p","html":"The fractions are handy in conversation, but they are not exact millimetres: one maker's 1/2 may differ slightly from another's.","disc":null,"rule_ref":null},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-p6","t":"p","html":"Italian makers, Beretta for example, use <a class=\"term\" href=\"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/star-marks/\">star marks</a>: five stars for cylinder, four for improved cylinder (1/4), three for modified (1/2), two for improved modified (3/4) and one for full. Other makers cut notches on the rim of the tube, use coloured bands or print the name (IC, M, LM).","disc":null,"rule_ref":null},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-p7","t":"tip","html":"The most reliable source is the marking on the choke itself or the gun's manual. If in doubt, a <a class=\"term\" href=\"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/gunsmith/\">gunsmith</a> can measure the constriction with a gauge in a minute.","disc":null,"rule_ref":null}],"notes":[],"phrases":[],"key_points":[{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-kp1","html":"Cylinder 0 · 1/8 ~0.1–0.15 mm · IC ~0.25 mm (1/4) · LM ~0.35–0.4 mm","checklist":false},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-kp2","html":"M ~0.5 mm (1/2) · IM ~0.65–0.75 mm (3/4) · Full ~0.9–1 mm (1/1)","checklist":false},{"k":"gear-chokes-kp3","html":"Fractions: quarter = improved cylinder, half = modified, three-quarter = improved modified, full.","checklist":false},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-kp4","html":"Stars: the more stars, the more open the choke.","checklist":false},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-kp5","html":"Values are approximate and differ between makers.","checklist":false}],"did_you_know":{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-dyk","html":"Modern competition barrels are often 'overbored': the inside is made slightly wider than the standard for the gauge. So the same choke with the same name can give a slightly different constriction in different guns: what counts is the difference between bore and choke."},"quiz":[{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-q1","q":"What is the approximate constriction of a modified (1/2) choke in 12 gauge?","options":[{"k":"a","text":"0 mm"},{"k":"b","text":"About 0.25 mm"},{"k":"c","text":"About 0.5 mm"},{"k":"d","text":"About 1 mm"}],"correct":"c","explain":"Modified is about 0.5 mm, or around 20 thou."},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-q2","q":"What does a choke marked with four stars mean on a Beretta gun?","options":[{"k":"a","text":"Full"},{"k":"b","text":"Improved cylinder (1/4)"},{"k":"c","text":"Modified (1/2)"},{"k":"d","text":"Improved modified (3/4)"}],"correct":"b","explain":"In the star system, the more stars the more open the choke: four stars is improved cylinder."},{"k":"chokes-names-and-marks-q4","q":"In European usage, which choke is a 'half'?","options":[{"k":"a","text":"Modified"},{"k":"b","text":"Improved cylinder"},{"k":"c","text":"Full"},{"k":"d","text":"Cylinder"}],"correct":"a","explain":"Half (1/2) is modified; quarter is improved cylinder, three-quarter is improved modified."}],"terms":[{"id":"choke","en":"Choke","local":"","label":"Choke","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/choke/"},{"id":"constriction","en":"Constriction","local":"","label":"Constriction","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/constriction/"},{"id":"cylinder","en":"Cylinder","local":"","label":"Cylinder","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/cylinder/"},{"id":"improved-cylinder","en":"Improved cylinder","local":"","label":"Improved cylinder","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/improved-cylinder/"},{"id":"light-modified","en":"Light modified","local":"","label":"Light modified","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/light-modified/"},{"id":"modified","en":"Modified","local":"","label":"Modified","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/modified/"},{"id":"improved-modified","en":"Improved modified","local":"","label":"Improved modified","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/improved-modified/"},{"id":"full-choke","en":"Full choke","local":"","label":"Full choke","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/full-choke/"},{"id":"star-marks","en":"Star marks","local":"","label":"Star marks","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/star-marks/"},{"id":"gunsmith","en":"Gunsmith","local":"","label":"Gunsmith","url":"https://juliabezdolna.github.io/clayaway/en/glossary/gunsmith/"}],"rule_refs":[],"sources":[{"title":"Briley — Understanding chokes: 12-gauge constriction chart (inches)","url":"https://www.briley.com/c-456-understanding-chokes.aspx","fitasc":false},{"title":"Beretta — choke tube identification: stars, notches and codes","url":"https://estore.beretta.com/en-gb/utility/choke-tubes-identification","fitasc":false}],"evidence":[],"more_in_en":0}
